  "preamble": "The shift from the voiced dental stop [d] to a glottal stop [ʔ] is a form of debuccalization. While less common than the [t] to [ʔ] shift, it occurs in rapid or informal speech in specific English dialects, particularly in word-final positions or before other consonants.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Debuccalization, Glottalization",
  "languageExamples": [
